# Cursor AI Rules for Glass Project

## Git Commit Messages

When generating commit messages, ALWAYS follow the Conventional Commits specification:

### Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer]
```

### Required Types

- `feat`: New feature (minor version bump)
- `fix`: Bug fix (patch version bump)
- `docs`: Documentation changes
- `style`: Code style/formatting (no code change)
- `refactor`: Code refactoring (no functional change)
- `perf`: Performance improvements
- `test`: Adding or updating tests
- `build`: Build system or dependency changes
- `ci`: CI/CD configuration changes
- `chore`: Other changes (no src/test files)
- `revert`: Revert previous commit

### Breaking Changes

For breaking changes, add `!` after type or add `BREAKING CHANGE:` in footer:

```
feat!: redesign API authentication
fix(api)!: remove deprecated endpoints
```

### Scope (Optional but Recommended)

Common scopes in this project:

- `asr`: Speech recognition related
- `llm`: Language model processing
- `api`: API endpoints
- `websocket`: WebSocket functionality
- `ui`: User interface
- `config`: Configuration
- `deps`: Dependencies

### Examples

✅ GOOD:

- `feat(asr): add Whisper integration`
- `fix(websocket): prevent connection timeout`
- `docs: update installation guide`
- `perf(llm): optimize prompt caching`
- `feat(api)!: change authentication to OAuth2`

❌ BAD:

- `added new feature`
- `Fix bug`
- `update code`
- `WIP`

### Special Cases

- Multiple changes: Split into separate commits
- Work in progress: Use `chore(wip): description` if necessary
- Merge commits: Let Git handle these automatically

## Code Style

### Python

- Follow PEP 8
- Use type hints for all function parameters and returns
- Write docstrings for public APIs
- Use descriptive variable names

### TypeScript/React

- Use functional components with hooks
- Prefer `const` over `let`
- Use TypeScript types/interfaces (no `any`)
- Follow existing code patterns

## General Guidelines

- Keep changes focused and atomic
- Write tests for new features
- Update documentation when needed
- Review your own changes before committing
